  "title": "Right Side View",
  "topic": "trees",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "trees / Level last",
  "statement": "# Right Side View\n\nThe tree is given as a level-order array `root`: the children of the entry at index `i` live at indices `2i+1` (left) and `2i+2` (right). An entry holding `null` means that child is absent and is skipped while traversing.\n\nImagine standing to the right of the tree and looking left: at each depth only the rightmost present node stays visible. Return those visible values from top to bottom. An empty tree shows nothing, so return `[]`.\n\nConstraints: `0 <= root.length <= 30` and `-100 <= root[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "root = [1,2,3,null,5,null,4]",
      "output": "[1,3,4]",
      "explanation": "Depth 0 shows 1; at depth 1 the rightmost node is 3; at depth 2 the candidates are 5 and 4, so 4 wins."
    },
    {
      "input": "root = [1,null,3]",
      "output": "[1,3]",
      "explanation": "The missing left child changes nothing on the right: 1 then 3 are visible."
    },
    {
      "input": "root = [1,2,null,3]",
      "output": "[1,2,3]",
      "explanation": "A left-leaning chain hides nothing from the right, so every level's single node is visible."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function rightSideView(root: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The visible node at each depth is the rightmost one.",
    "Visit right children before left children.",
    "Keep the first value you meet at every new depth."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Depth-first walk that always visits the right child before the left, recording the first node seen at each depth.",
    "why_optimal": "Each node is visited once and only one value per depth is stored, so time is linear and extra space follows the height. On 29 PAF-verified cases the judge measured the brute force at 0.24 ms and the optimal at 0.14 ms (about 1.7x faster).",
    "pitfalls": "Record a depth only the first time you reach it; later left-side arrivals at the same depth stay hidden."
  },
